Agent service — design decisions #agents #cloud #design

The cloud Nexus **agent service**: Waldo plus the specialist agents, the runtime
that runs them on a user's nexus, and the Studio that manages them. This file is
the settled design, captured from a voice grill-me session (`scratch/grill-waldo`,
22 decisions). It is **our cloud product** — dogfood workbooks deployed via the
DeployKit, NOT baked into the open-standard nexus image. See [[the-line]].

## Runtime — how an agent runs

- **Unit of execution** — one-shot **function call** from the user's view (a clean
  start/finish), optionally fired by automation (cron). Not a user-managed daemon.
- **Where it runs** — **BEAM by default** (Elixir scheduler does orchestration +
  scheduling). **WASM** only for specific tool/capability logic that wants
  sandboxing or is untrusted/heavy. Agent ≈ [[agent-is-a-sandboxed-function]].
- **Concurrency** — each run is a **separate, supervised BEAM process**; one run
  crashing never takes down another run or the nexus. One nexus per org
  ([[one-nexus-per-org]]); a second wholly-separate nexus is the scale-out unit.
- **Visibility** — runs **stream tokens + lifecycle events onto the #event bus**;
  Studio (and any hook) watches reactively. This is the run-visibility interface.
- **Failure model** — **no blind auto-restart.** An agent validates within its
  `flow`; on a problem it **files an issue** (git/.work model) for later repair by
  a human or another agent. Failure is proactive + declarative, not OTP-restart.

## The agent file — one `.work` `agent` block

- **One block, complete contract** — `prompt + tools + grant + limit + flow`. **No
  separate `server` brain block.** Self-contained identity → logic → caps.
- **Flow** — technically optional (a trivial reactive agent can omit it) but
  **strongly discouraged to omit**: `flow` is the perceive→plan→act loop +
  validation + task management that makes real agency work.
- **Tools** — declared as capability atoms in a **`grant` list**, resolved against
  the **Effects registry** ([[hooks-events-effects-architecture]]) and enforced at
  call time.
- **Limit** — caps **tokens, wall-clock, sub-agent depth, and cost** — but applied
  **sparingly / carefully**: over-tight limits kill legitimate long runs.
- **Default agents** — live in **`dogfood/cloud/agents/`** as `.work` files,
  **seeded into every new nexus**. Users fully own/modify them on disk.
- **Customisation** — users **fork & edit** default agents; **git merge** handles
  upstream updates. No template/instance abstraction.

## Waldo — the manager

- **Remit** — the **expert on the Nexus framework itself**: orchestrator +
  delegator, AND agent-writer + diagnostician + default **workhorse**. He both
  routes work and does it.
- **Singleton** per nexus. **Session memory** lives in a **notes table** in the
  tenant DB (persistent cross-session context).
- **Handoffs** — Waldo **brokers** handoffs between specialists; data passes as
  **ephemeral handoff documents** = `.work` files with file references. Waldo
  stays in control of the orchestration flow.

## Scheduling

- Agents schedule future work via a **`hook` `trigger`** (cron / at / every),
  reusing **Nexus.Scheduler** ([[time-scheduling-lane]]). The **nexus owns** the
  tasks; an agent may schedule for **itself, another agent (with permission), or an
  app**. Ownership is viewable in Studio.
- **Wake behaviour** — overdue tasks generally **fire on wake**, BUT after extended
  downtime do **not** flood the nexus with a backlog. *(Open: define "extended" +
  the backlog policy.)*

## Sub-agents

- **Results are structured** — returned as **`.work`** response/handoff documents,
  not free text. Predictable, robust passing.
- **Studio view** — sub-agent runs are **collapsed under the parent** by default;
  full depth preserved for debugging.

## Issues

- Agent-filed and human-filed issues are **stored the same way** (the .work/git
  model), but the **Studio separates + triages** them with dedicated views. (Note:
  this is the *nexus* issue store — NOT bd, which is local-only/git-ignored.)
  *(Open: exact physical home of the issue store — git-indexed .work vs DB.)*

## Studio — managing it all

- **Starting a task** — both **chat with Waldo** (he delegates) AND **pick a
  specialist directly**. Cater to orchestration-first and know-what-I-want users.
- **HITL approvals** — surface in an **inbox of pending approvals**, asynchronous,
  rather than interrupting the active run. *(Open: whether the inbox grows into a
  full notifications center across approvals / delegated-task status / issues.)*

## Specialist agents

The voice interview degraded before this round, so these are **recommended
decisions** (grounded in repo canon), not yet founder-ratified — flag any you'd
steer differently.

### DeckAgent — presentations via wasm Reveal.js

- **Output** — a **`.work` surface** that renders the deck (a folder with
  `index.work` → mounted at its path, WYSIWYG), **not** a standalone artifact.
  The deck IS a workbook; Reveal.js is the render target of its `client` island.
- **Input** — a **prose brief** (most natural), optionally a structured `design`
  block for tight control. **Iterative** by default: it drafts, you react, it
  revises — same chat-with-preview loop as the Studio split-view.
- **Home** — lands in the active workspace tree (e.g. `<ws>/decks/<name>/`),
  **versioned + committed** like any workspace file (nexus = git remote).

### VideoAgent — via the wasm-video package

- **Job** — **render from a script/storyboard** first (assemble clips + captions +
  audio into a timeline); editing existing footage is a later mode. Input is a
  `.work` storyboard the agent authors/edits.
- **Execution** — rendering is heavy, so it runs as a **scheduled job** (a `flow`
  step), **not** inline in the request. Progress streams onto the **#event bus**
  (Studio shows a progress meter); the finished file lands in the tree + a
  `#event` fires on completion (notify the user).

### ResearchAgent — organized findings, not a doc dump

- **Organising scheme** — by the **active ask / project** (the scope you're working
  in), with a **topic taxonomy inside it** — folders of `.work` notes, cross-linked
  by `[[backlinks]]` + `#tags`. The repo structure mirrors the project, not a flat
  pile.
- **Integrations (v1)** — **web search** + **a small set of MCP tools** (the
  integrations OAuth lane). Arbitrary data sources are post-v1. Keep v1 scope tight:
  search → fetch → organize.
- **Pipelines** — yes: a **scheduled recurring `flow` + `trigger`** (gather →
  dedupe → organize) reusing Nexus.Scheduler — the "research pipeline."
- **Queryability** — primary is **well-named `.work` files + backlinks/tags** (the
  source of truth); an **embedding index is a derived accelerator** layered on top,
  never the store itself. (Pinecone-style index is an optional derived cache.)

## Capabilities, cost, limits

- **Default deny-all** — an agent gets only the grants it declares; resolved vs the
  Effects registry. **Grant escalation requires approval** — surfaces in the same
  Studio approvals inbox as HITL.
- **Cost** — metered **per run, attributed up to per-agent and per-org**. Hitting a
  budget **pauses** the agent (resumable, lands in the approvals inbox) rather than
  hard-failing — matches the "limits applied sparingly" decision.
- **External spend** — agents CAN spend on external APIs (model tokens, search)
  under a **per-org spend cap**, surfaced as an **upsell knob** ([[upsells]]) —
  config-driven via `Nexus.Config`, neutral defaults in the runtime ([[the-line]]).

## Bootstrap & the line

- **Our cloud product, NOT the open standard** — Waldo + the specialists are
  **dogfood workbooks deployed via the DeployKit**, exactly like a customer. The
  open nexus image ships **none** of them. ([[the-line]])
- **Seeding** — when a new org's nexus comes up, the **DeployKit seeds**
  `dogfood/cloud/agents/*.work` into the tree (committed, so they're forkable);
  the dashboard does not special-case them.
- **v1 'done' bar** — **Waldo + ONE specialist (DeckAgent) running a real task
  end-to-end** through the Studio (start → stream → result in tree), with the
  runtime + agent-file contract + issue-filing in place. The other two specialists
  + full scheduling/sub-agent UI follow once that spine is proven.

## Still open (genuinely undecided — flag for next pass)

- Issue-store physical home: git-indexed `.work` vs DB table (`wb-35ma`).
- Wake backlog policy after extended downtime (define "extended").
- Whether the approvals inbox grows into a full notifications center.
- Cross-agent scheduling permission model details.
